{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And you shall see it.</b> And it was not sufficient what He had already said, ״וּרְאֵה אֶת הָאָרֶץ״, according to their statement, may their memory be for blessing (Sifrei Part I 136), that HaShem showed him a miraculous vision such as the power of natural sight cannot see in the light of the sun, except by the hidden light, as is known. And therefore He promised him and said to him <b>וְרָאִיתָה אֹתָהּ</b>, for the light of life would shine before him. And likewise you will find that the verse says (Deuteronomy 34:1) ״וַיַּרְאֵהוּ ה׳״ etc., to call attention to what is said." "<b>Also you, just as</b> etc. This is explained according to their words, may their memory be for blessing (Yalkut Shimoni, beginning of parashat Masei), who said that Aaron said to Moses: Had I known this, I would have desired the thing beforehand. Therefore He said <b>גַּם אָתָּה</b> adjacent to the statement <b>וְנֶאֱסַפְתָּ</b>, to tell him that he should not recoil from the gathering, for He promises him that he too will desire and want to be gathered when he sees the sweetness of what is grasped at the hour of death. And this is your sign: ״כַּאֲשֶׁר נֶאֱסַף אַהֲרֹן אָחִיךָ״ — for he said to you that if he had known etc.; see, then, that even the desire and the will agree to be gathered. Further, He intends to alert him to the mitzvah incumbent upon the righteous at the hour of death, which is that one should justify the judgment upon oneself and desire the decree of the King to be fulfilled. And this is what He says: <b>וְנֶאֱסַפְתָּ</b> etc. <b>גַּם אָתָּה</b> — meaning, even you will agree to be gathered, in the manner of the verse (Psalms 31:6) ״בְּיָדְךָ אַפְקִיד״." "And His saying <b>כַּאֲשֶׁר נֶאֱסַף אַהֲרֹן</b> etc., is explained according to their statement, may their memory be for blessing (Yalkut there), that Moses said to Aaron: ״Aaron my brother, is it accepted upon you to die?״ He said to him: ״Yes.״ He said to him: ״Let us go up the mountain,״ and immediately they went up etc., until here. And this is what is alluded to in the statement <b>כַּאֲשֶׁר נֶאֱסַף אַהֲרֹן</b> — meaning, that he too agreed to the gathering. And the reason for this matter, that HaShem acts in this order, is because He does not wish to take the soul of the righteous except with its consent, because of the abundant love of His beloved, and also because through this the complete felicity is perfected, as stated (Deuteronomy 6:5) ״וּבְכָל נַפְשְׁךָ״."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:15:35.262926494Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}